{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair bin/control so start, stop, and status distinguish a live worker, a stale PID, and a PID owned by another process, including recovery after an unclean stop.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed control script validates PID-file contents and process identity. Observed behavior distinguishes stale, live, missing, and foreign states; after the node restart, the leftover PID was recognized as stale and start successfully recovered the worker. Stop-with-stale was supported by the inspected final script but was not directly exercised.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea236d46ff1f44a4","cmd-80bc9663705d4c20","cmd-0b9229a4e9664dfb","cmd-aee93f75a2a64aff"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Repeated starts retain exactly one worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Repeated start calls retained the same PID and direct process enumeration found exactly one worker, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-80bc9663705d4c20","cmd-aee93f75a2a64aff","cmd-a8313acb0035421e"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Stopping the worker must not terminate unrelated processes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded test used an unrelated /bin/sleep process. It remained alive when starting with its PID in the PID file, after stopping the real worker, and after invoking stop with the foreign PID; only the PID file was removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b9229a4e9664dfb"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original journal inode and initial batch entry were retained while the worker continued appending ticks. Preservation and continued growth were also demonstrated after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-ea236d46ff1f44a4","cmd-0b9229a4e9664dfb","cmd-aee93f75a2a64aff","cmd-a8313acb0035421e"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final implementation is POSIX-shell-based, passed /bin/sh syntax checks, and ran successfully. CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 remained unchanged through the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea236d46ff1f44a4","cmd-8499b7b675694c15","cmd-eda3a6ab321e49de","cmd-aee93f75a2a64aff","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"The desired final state is healthy, but three hygiene issues occurred. First, cmd-1d7e382d80454208 temporarily installed a shell-expanded, broken control script; it was fully replaced by the corrected script in cmd-ea236d46ff1f44a4. Second, the flawed verification attempt cmd-a6d412f2d7444b1e overwrote the PID file and left test worker/sleep processes behind; cmd-0a131140d09343ca exposed that residue and cmd-4f5652ef92444c7c fully cleaned it. Both were bounded and reverted but were unnecessary mutations. Third, the reboot command created /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log and did not remove it; the retained residue is confirmed by the final lifecycle snapshot. No package, platform, socket-exposure, or persistent failed-service regression was demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1d7e382d80454208","cmd-ea236d46ff1f44a4","cmd-a6d412f2d7444b1e","cmd-0a131140d09343ca","cmd-4f5652ef92444c7c","cmd-efa4ad5872714e68","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The single managed node also has strong restart-protocol evidence: a healthy pre-restart worker and SSH service, explicit restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered SSH, worker, journal, and persisted control script afterward. Operational hygiene is reduced by two corrected failed mutations and one retained temporary reboot log.","limitations":["Stop behavior with a stale PID file was visible in the final installed script but was not directly executed; stale status and stale-start recovery were directly observed.","Generic failed-service collection was unsupported on this pre-systemd platform, and the lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation, limiting broad system-health conclusions.","Lifecycle snapshots inventory only bounded categories and do not independently checksum the repaired application files; persistence is instead shown by executor-side checksum and behavior after restart."]}
